-- ================================================================
-- guardians.vocabulary — the data sorts and the non-model tools.
--
-- Nothing here is a new language feature. `Text` is an ordinary sort
-- with an ordinary type parameter; the only unusual thing is WHAT the
-- parameter ranges over — a trust level rather than an element type.
-- Because anthill type arguments carry logical variables and unify,
-- that one choice is the whole information-flow mechanism.
-- ================================================================

-- The trust lattice. Two points is all this example needs; an n-point
-- lattice would cost O(n^2) coercions (docs/design/high-level-api.md D2).
enum guardians.TrustLevel
  entity Untrusted            -- came from outside; may be attacker-authored
  entity Public               -- cleared to leave the system
end

-- Text, labelled by where it came from. `Trust` is the parameter SLOT;
-- `TrustLevel` is the sort its arguments inhabit.
--
-- WHY A PARAMETER, AND WHICH HALF OF IT IS A WORKAROUND.
--
-- The parameter is NOT a workaround. It is the only way an inner label becomes
-- visible to a signature: without it `Text` is one single type, and
-- `send_email(body: Text[Public])` — the declaration that refuses the article's
-- exfiltration — cannot be written at all, because there is no slot to put
-- `Public` into. A label sitting in a field is unreachable from a signature,
-- and labels are erased at run time, so there is no later opportunity to check
-- one. This parameter stays.
--
-- The `= ?` IS a workaround, and it should go away. `sort Trust = ?` is
-- UNCONSTRAINED, so the slot admits anything at all — `Text[Int64]` loads
-- clean, measured. A `requires IsLevel[T = Trust]` does not bind it either, and
-- the reason is narrower than it looks: the only use-site enforcement that
-- exists is hard-wired to `anthill.prelude.Eq` (WI-644/WI-835), so the clause
-- is never consulted. Either way `TrustLevel` above is what these arguments are
-- MEANT to inhabit, not what they are required to — the lattice is a convention
-- held up by the pairings written below, and `Text[Publik]` would be a fresh
-- type rather than a typo.
--
-- Tracked as WI-20260822-T70A2, designed as proposal 062. What arrives is NOT a
-- bound. A bound would be `<:`, which drags in variance, transitivity and
-- higher-kinded parameters that none of this needs; what 062 settles on is a
-- GOAL, discharged when the parameter binds. These three declarations — here,
-- `Message` below, and `Prompt` in lib/llm.anthill — then read
--
--   sort Trust = ?
--   requires is_entity_of(Trust, TrustLevel)
--
-- with the admissible set DERIVED from the enum above rather than restated
-- beside it. This note goes with them.
--
-- Not a security hole either way, and measured: the suite passes 15/15 with the
-- slot unconstrained, the exfiltration refusal among them, because a sink
-- demands a LITERAL label and `Text[Int64]` reaches nothing. What is unsound is
-- only the claim one level up, that the parameter ranges over a lattice. It
-- ranges over everything.
enum guardians.Text
  import anthill.prelude.{String}
  sort Trust = ?
  entity text(raw: String)
end

-- A message, labelled the same way: subject and body from one message
-- are equally trusted.
enum guardians.Message
  import guardians.{Text, Address, MessageId}
  sort Trust = ?
  entity message(
    id       : MessageId,
    from     : Address,
    reply_to : Address,
    subject  : Text[Trust],
    body     : Text[Trust])
end

-- `Model` IS A CAPABILITY AND NOTHING ELSE: the argument of `Permission[Model]`,
-- "this operation ACQUIRES the authority to consult a model"
-- (kernel-language.md §5.5, proposal 064). It is not an effect kind and is not
-- registered as one.
--
-- IT WORE TWO HATS ONCE, and the second is worth recording because losing it was
-- the point. `Model` was also a project-defined EFFECT LABEL — "this operation
-- consults a model" — so that `-Model` could deny consultation to a checker
-- handed an `Llm` it should not have. `docs/design/effects.md` rejected that hat
-- on its own terms ("semantics: `{External, Error}`, and nothing more"), and
-- `prelude/external.anthill` forbids the shape outright: "ONE effect, not one per
-- capability (no Http / Db / Forge effects) — what distinguishes external
-- capabilities is AUTHORITY, which lives in CARRIERS".
--
-- WHAT MADE IT DELETABLE was making the model's ANSWER unreadable rather than the
-- call unavailable. `LlmOutput` (llm.anthill) is sealed, so a component that
-- consults a model receives a token it can neither project nor match: the call
-- teaches it nothing, and being steered requires reading the answer. The row
-- label was approximating that property by forbidding the call; the type now
-- holds it directly, and one denial — of ACQUISITION — is all that is left.
sort guardians.Model
end

-- A SUB-CAPABILITY. A frontier model is a model, so `FrontierModel <: Model`,
-- and that is the whole declaration — `provides` on a constructor-less sort is
-- the is-a.
--
-- IT EXISTS TO BE DENIED. A denial is DOWNWARD-CLOSED in the capability:
-- `-Permission[Model]` forbids `Permission[X]` for every `X <: Model`, because
-- acquiring a frontier model IS acquiring a model. So a generated checker cannot
-- evade its denial by naming a narrower capability
-- (rejected/frontier_checker.anthill).
--
-- THAT IS ENTAILMENT, NOT THE DECLARED CONTRAVARIANCE, and the two run OPPOSITE
-- ways — worth stating because the names invite the wrong reading.
-- `fact Contravariant(sort: Permission, param: T)` is the SUBSUMPTION rule: a
-- spec granting `Permission[FrontierModel]` accepts an implementation that takes
-- only `Permission[Model]`, because it asks less. The closure above is the other
-- direction and runs COVARIANTLY in the capability; it needed its own rule in the
-- kernel (`typing::permission_entails`), which is the one thing proposal 064
-- predicted would come free and did not. See
-- `stdlib/anthill/prelude/permission.anthill`.
--
-- NOT registered as an effect kind: it is a capability and never a bare label,
-- which is the separation 064 asks for.
sort guardians.FrontierModel
  import guardians.{Model}
  provides Model
end

-- THE OUTBOX CAPABILITY — the authority to mail OUTSIDE the organisation.
--
-- A capability, not an effect kind (like `FrontierModel` above, and unlike
-- `Model` / `Filesystem`): it is only ever the argument of `Permission[…]`.
--
-- What makes it worth having is that the permission `send_email` demands is
-- CONDITIONAL (proposal 048's guarded effects, on 064's label). The article
-- states the policy as *"forbid data flow from fetch_email's result to the body
-- parameter of send_email WITH AN EXTERNAL EMAIL ADDRESS AS THE TARGET"*, and
-- the target half of that is a property of one argument at one call — exactly
-- what a guard reads. So mailing a colleague needs no authority at all, and
-- mailing outside needs `Permission[Outbox]`, decided at LOAD from the address
-- the call actually passes.
sort guardians.Outbox
end

-- A second project effect: touching the filesystem. Present so the row checks
-- have a label to widen BY that is not `Modify`, and it stays after C9 was
-- fixed: the two widenings travel DIFFERENT ARMS of one check — a named label
-- is compared as a TYPE, a `Modify` target as a RESOURCE (§5.6's frame
-- condition) — so `rejected/wide_row.anthill` and
-- `rejected/wide_row_modify.anthill` each catch a program the other does not.
sort guardians.Filesystem
end

namespace guardians
  import anthill.prelude.{String, List, Unit, Error, External, Effect}
  import guardians.TrustLevel.{Untrusted, Public}
  import anthill.prelude.{Permission}
  import guardians.{Text, Message, Model, Filesystem, Outbox}

  -- The project's one effect KIND. `Model` and `FrontierModel` are deliberately
  -- absent: both are CAPABILITIES (arguments of `Permission[…]`) and never bare
  -- row labels. `Model` WAS registered here, and the reason it no longer is, is
  -- `LlmOutput` (llm.anthill): a sealed wrapper makes model output unreadable, so
  -- a component that consults a model learns nothing from it and cannot be
  -- steered by it — which is the property `-Model` approximated by forbidding
  -- the call. With the property held by a type, the label bought nothing, and
  -- the prelude's rule against per-capability effects
  -- (`prelude/external.anthill`: "no Http / Db / Forge effects") is restored.
  -- `Permission` itself is registered by the prelude.
  fact Effect[T = Filesystem]

  entity MessageId(value: String)
  entity Address(local: String, domain: String)
  entity Mailbox(owner: Address)

  -- The report SPLITS enumeration from prose. `items` is one Verdict per
  -- fetched message, built by iterating the list `fetch_mail` returned —
  -- the model never constructs it and therefore cannot drop a row from
  -- it. That is what defeats the injection's CONCEALMENT half, which
  -- freezing a tool sequence does not touch (docs/design/two-flows.md §1.3).
  entity Verdict(message: MessageId, label: String)
  entity Report(items: List[Verdict], summary: LlmOutput[Text[Untrusted]])

  -- ---------------- tools that consult no model ----------------

  -- The source. The label is fixed by the SIGNATURE, which is the only
  -- place it can enter (measured: C6).
  operation fetch_mail(box: Mailbox) -> List[Message[Untrusted]]
    effects {External, Error}

  -- Projection: a message's body carries the message's label.
  --
  -- IT IS ORDINARY API, and this comment used to claim the opposite. It said an
  -- agent CANNOT write this projection — that both spellings it would reach for
  -- are refused, so the trusted vocabulary has to supply what the agent cannot
  -- express. Both halves of that were wrong, one per spelling, and both are now
  -- measured in
  -- `guardians_test::an_agent_can_inline_the_body_projection`:
  --
  --   msgs.map(lambda m -> m.body).collect()                      LOADS
  --   msgs.map(lambda m -> match m case message(i,f,r,s,b) -> b)
  --     .collect()                                                LOADS
  --
  -- The dot spelling's `<unresolved receiver>.body` was an artefact of the probe
  -- that reported it: the substitution wrote `Iterable.map(…)` into a file that
  -- does not import `Iterable`, and the refusal was about the missing import.
  -- The match spelling's `expected ?Dst` was real and is WI-20260829-9TGP7,
  -- fixed. What each spelling DOES still need is the `collect` — `map` yields a
  -- lazy `MappedStream` and `summarize` wants a `List` (WI-20260829-N01PY, open).
  --
  -- SO WHY IT STAYS. `docs/design/measured.md` settled this under C7 before the
  -- fix and the answer did not change: a message's body genuinely IS a
  -- projection and the label genuinely does ride along it, so the operation
  -- earns its place as API. It is no longer the thing standing between the
  -- design and a laundered label — the inlined spellings preserve `Untrusted`,
  -- which the test above pins by re-running the article's attack through them.
  --
  -- WHY IT WAS WRITTEN OUT ORIGINALLY, which is a different reason again: it
  -- surfaced C7, as a DISCIPLINE keeping every label-polymorphic operation
  -- reachable only through arguments of exactly its declared sort — because
  -- until WI-RKMD4 a sort mismatch against a parameter containing a type
  -- variable was accepted SILENTLY, leaving the label variable free to bind
  -- `Public` at the sink. The typer refuses that now (including a mis-projected
  -- `List[Text[?t]]` handed to this signature), so the discipline is enforced
  -- rather than conventional.
  operation bodies_of(msgs: List[Message[?t]])
      -> List[Text[?t]]

  -- One Verdict per message, in the order given. This is the operation an
  -- agent MUST call to satisfy `ensures mentions_all(result)`: the enumeration
  -- is derived from the fetched list, so a generated body cannot drop a row
  -- from it, which is what defeats the injection's CONCEALMENT half. An agent
  -- that fabricates `items` instead fails the postcondition.
  operation verdicts_of(msgs: List[Message[?t]]) -> List[Verdict]

  -- The sink. `Public` is FIXED, so nothing untrusted can reach it.
  -- WHO THIS ADDRESS BELONGS TO — DECLARED here, ASSERTED nowhere in `lib/`.
  -- Which domain is "ours" is a DEPLOYMENT's fact, not the library's, exactly as
  -- `safety.anthill` says of `Checked` and `mailbox.anthill` says of the inbox:
  -- the relation is the library's, the rows are a deployment's. The rows live in
  -- `fixtures/mailbox.anthill`.
  --
  -- A bodyless `rule` is proposal 061's DECLARATION rather than a clause, so with
  -- no deployment loaded this relation is empty and EVERY address is external —
  -- which is the safe default: an unconfigured organisation grants nothing.
  rule in_org(?a)
  rule external_addr(?a) :- not(in_org(?a))

  -- THE SINK, GATED ON ITS TARGET. `Permission[Outbox]` is demanded only when the
  -- recipient is outside the organisation: at the call the argument is
  -- substituted into the guard, and the label is dropped when the guard's
  -- negation is constructively proved (§5.5). So
  --
  --   send_email(to: Address(local: "boss", domain: "ourcorp.com"), …)  nothing
  --   send_email(to: Address(local: "it", domain: "othercorp.com"), …)  Permission[Outbox]
  --   send_email(to: <anything not written inline>, …)                  Permission[Outbox]
  --
  -- THE RULE IS NARROWER THAN "PROVABLY INTERNAL", and stating it loosely was a
  -- real error in an earlier draft of this comment. The guard is refuted only
  -- where the checker can prove `in_org(to)` from the argument TERM AT THE CALL,
  -- so the address must be written INLINE. Measured: the identical literal
  -- let-bound one line earlier is REFUSED
  -- (rejected/letbound_recipient.anthill) — `refute_guard` proves the negation
  -- over Γ, and a `let` deposits an equation SLD does not use to ground the goal,
  -- so the double negation flounders and §5.5 conservatively keeps the effect.
  --
  -- So the operative policy is: A GENERATED AGENT MAY MAIL ONLY AN ADDRESS
  -- WRITTEN LITERALLY, INLINE, AT THE CALL — and matching a deployment's `in_org`
  -- rows. Everything else (an external literal, a let-bound one, a computed
  -- address, one read out of a message) needs an authority `Triage.run` never
  -- grants. That is sound, and stricter than intended in the let-bound case,
  -- which is a typer limit rather than a policy decision: the bound value IS
  -- statically known. Worth a ticket; not worth pretending it is by design.
  --
  -- THE CONSEQUENCE FOR A GENERATED AGENT is the whole point: `Triage.run`'s spec
  -- row grants no `Permission[Outbox]`, so an implementation can neither declare
  -- one (a widening) nor perform one (an undeclared effect). No generated triage
  -- can mail outside the organisation, and that is a property of the SPEC rather
  -- than of any particular agent.
  --
  -- INDEPENDENT OF THE `Text[Trust]` LABEL, which the article's policy names in
  -- the same sentence. The two halves are measured by two fixtures, neither of
  -- which catches the other's program: `rejected/leak.anthill` violates the FLOW
  -- (an `Untrusted` summary reaching a `Public` parameter) and
  -- `rejected/outbox.anthill` violates the TARGET (a literal `Public` body, no
  -- flow at all, external recipient). In `leak` the label error is reported and
  -- the effect leg does not run, so it is NOT observably caught twice — the two
  -- are independent by fixture rather than by double diagnosis.
  operation send_email(to: Address, body: Text[Public]) -> Unit
    effects {External, Error, (Permission[Outbox] :- external_addr(to))}

  -- NO TRANSITIONS BETWEEN LEVELS, and their removal is a finding rather than a
  -- tidy-up. `widen` (Public -> Untrusted, free) and `declassify` (the other way,
  -- guarded by an `Approval` token) were both declared and NEITHER WAS EVER
  -- CALLED — not in `lib/`, not in a fixture, not in a test. `declassify` could
  -- not be: nothing in the example mints an `Approval`, so no program could write
  -- the call even if it wanted to.
  --
  -- WHAT MADE THEM WORSE THAN UNUSED is that a free route exists beside them:
  -- `text(raw: u.raw)` re-labels any `Text[Untrusted]` as `Text[Public]` in one
  -- line and loads clean, because `text`'s constructor is public and the label is
  -- phantom. So the guarded door was unreachable while the window stood open, and
  -- declaring the door suggested a discipline the example does not enforce.
  --
  -- What the lattice still does, and it is measured: it pins a value's label AT A
  -- SINK. `send_email(body: Text[Public])` refuses `Text[Untrusted]`
  -- (`rejected/leak.anthill`). What it does not do is confine how a label CHANGES.
  -- Sealing `Text` the way `LlmOutput` is sealed would close that, at the cost of
  -- moving labelling to the boundary — `fixtures/mailbox.anthill` builds
  -- `Text[Untrusted]` in FACTS, which cannot call a smart constructor (measured:
  -- 10 sites).

  -- The recipient is chosen WITHOUT consulting a model, which defeats the
  -- article's attack at its root: the injection's whole strategy is to make a
  -- model name `it@othercorp.com`.
  --
  -- THE EMPTY ROW IS THE CLAIM, and it is stronger than the `-Model` this used to
  -- carry. A pure row admits no `External`, no `Permission[Model]` — so this body
  -- cannot mint a model, and cannot be handed one it could use, since the only
  -- thing a model returns is an `LlmOutput` nothing here can read. `-Model`
  -- denied one route; `{}` denies every route there is.
  operation choose_recipient(r: Report, box: Mailbox) -> Address
    effects {}
end
